**Decision 2 — engine and model for Dave.**
Facts:
- Insights ≈ 93 KB ≈ 25k tokens today; ≈40–50 KB/day growth; 1M tokens ≈ 80–100 working days away.
- Engine research: Claude Opus/Sonnet/Haiku 5.5 1M window (R1, R2), Haiku 5x over 100k (R3), Opus $4/$20, Sonnet $2/$10, cache reads 5% (R1, R4). Gemini 3.8 Flash 1,048,576 input, $0.75/$3.75 to end 2026 (R6, R7). deepseek-flash 1M, cheap (R9), off-peak half (R10). MiniMax 1M (R11, R12). gpt-6.1-sol input caps 922k (R15); Codex 0% quota today.
- Anthropic: accuracy degrades as context grows (R5) — context rot.
- Cost of one 25k-token call: Opus $0.10, Sonnet $0.05, gpt-6.1-sol $0.05, Gemini 3.8 Flash $0.019, Haiku $0.0025, deepseek-flash $0.0075 peak.

The plan says Dario (control) runs on the same engine as Dave. So Dave's engine is the decision.

My rules for this:
- #1 Reckon before committing: compare the engines on the five matters/seven questions. The reckoning: cost, window, quality, availability.
- #7 Deception / #10 avoid solid strike empty: don't load all 62; load related categories (the design already does).
- #4 Know self and other: Dave knowing himself (his load) vs the engine's weakness (context rot R5).
- #2 Make yourself unbeatable first: cache and index; fail-safe if engine unavailable.
- #8 Value victory not duration; supply from the enemy: cost per question; cache reads at 5% (R1) means the fixed insight prefix should be cached.
- #5 Move only on advantage: don't pay Opus $0.10 when deepseek is $0.0075 or Gemini $0.019, unless quality demands.
- #6 Mix harm into benefit: state the harm of each choice.
- #14 Choose by ratio of forces: 25k today vs 1M window → all engines hold it.
- #20 Judge by the land unit, the tax, what they breed: cost per question (tax) and what the choice breeds (context rot, lock-in).

Decision 2 answer: Today all engines hold 25k easily (context-windows digest). So the choice is by cost, quality (context rot), and the same-engine requirement with Dario. The plan says Dario same engine as Dave — so pick one engine for both.

What's the right engine? Given:
- Claude Sonnet 5.5: 1M, same rate across, cache reads 5%, $0.05/call at 25k, strong reasoning. Context rot applies (R5) but moderate.
- Haiku 5.5: cheapest ($0.0025) but 5x over 100k — a trap once insights grow past 100k (~in 2-3 days at 40-50KB/day!). Actually 100k tokens ≈ 400KB ≈ 8-10 working days at 40-50KB/day. Wait: 40-50 KB/day ≈ 10-12.5k tokens/day. 100k tokens / 11k ≈ 9 days. So Haiku's price step bites within ~9 working days. That's a strong #20 "what they breed" observation. Haiku is cheap now but grows 5x at 100k. Also Haiku is weakest.
- Gemini 3.8 Flash: 1,048,576 input, $0.75/$3.75 (R7), ~$0.019/call at 25k, cheapest of the capable ones except deepseek. But quality/long-context unknown (gap in context-windows: no long-context quality statements from Google).
- deepseek-flash: 1M, $0.15-0.30/M input, $0.0075/call peak, off-peak half (R9, R10). Very cheap. This is the DSH engine (the current harness). Quality: it's the model I'm running on. Hmm.
- Opus: best quality presumably but $0.10/call and 4x Sonnet.
- MiniMax: 1M, price not found (R14 unproven), suggests 512K.
- gpt-6.1-sol: input caps 922k (R15), so cannot hold a 1M file — dies last; and Codex 0% quota.

But wait — the decision's real risk is not cost per se at 25k; it's the growth to 1M and context rot. The window matters at 80-100 days. And the brief wants "when the choice must be revisited".

My recommendation: choose **Claude Sonnet 5.5** (1M, no price step, cache at 5% → the insight prefix cached), or **deepseek-flash** for cost. Which serves the use cases and vision best?

The vision is about insight making a person powerful. U3: load fits with room for question and answer. The critical hidden cost is context rot (R5) — simply fitting 1M is not enough; quality degrades. So the choice should be: keep the *loaded* set small (categories, index) so no engine ever needs 1M, rather than picking an engine for a 1M file. That's my #10 (avoid the solid, strike the empty) and #12 (seek it in 勢/position, not demand of people): change the loading position, not the model.

But Decision 2 explicitly asks which engine/model runs Dave. I'll recommend: **Sonnet 5.5** as the default for Dave (and Dario), because (a) 1M with no price step across the window (R4), (b) cache reads at 5% (R1) so the stable insight prefix is cheap across many questions, (c) it is a capable reasoner rather than the cheap tier, and the whole point is comparing rules+insights, so a weak engine would confound the measurement. Cost ≈ $0.05/question at 25k (researcher's arithmetic). Revisit when: the loaded set for a single question exceeds ~100k tokens (the Haiku step and the context-rot zone), or when the projected load approaches ~200-300k (Anthropic's R5 caution), or monthly cost. Actually the choice must be revisited when the *loaded* per-question set (not the whole file) crosses a threshold. And at 1M projected (80-100 days), gpt-6.1-sol is eliminated and the window question becomes real.

Hmm, but should I recommend DeepSeek because it's cheap and 1M? Let me think about "same engine as Dario" and the measurement. The Dave review showed Dave's value is measuring/predicting. The point of Dario is a plain control on the same engine to remove the tool confound. So the engine must support both a rules+insights seat and a plain seat. Any can.

Let me weigh with my rules. #1: reckon on five matters — I don't have the seven comparisons exactly, but I have cost, window, long-context quality, availability, and the fact the engine must run both seats. The research labels: Opus/Sonnet/Haiku/Gemini/deepseek/MiniMax verified windows; gpt-6.1-sol 922k input cap. Quality: only Anthropic's context-rot statement (R5), no long-context quality from others.

Given uncertainty in quality across engines, my #4 says know self and other; the gap is that long-context quality is unmeasured for Google/DeepSeek/OpenAI (context-windows Gaps). So I should answer conditionally: "if cost is the binding constraint, deepseek-flash; if quality/reliability is, Sonnet 5.5". The brief wants a single recommendation though.

Let me choose Sonnet 5.5, with reasoning:
- #1: the reckoning must include the *measurement goal*. Dave vs Dario is an experiment; using the cheapest engine risks a floor effect (both fail for engine reasons), confounding the rules+insights effect. So the engine must be capable.
- #4: Sonnet's window 1M with no price step (R4) and cache 5% (R1) — known; deepseek's long-context quality is unmeasured (gap).
- #8: supply from enemy — cache reads at 5% mean the fixed prefix is near-free on reuse; the marginal cost is the question+answer. Sonnet ~$0.05/call.
- #14: 25k today is tiny for 1M; no need to optimize for the 1M case yet.
- #20: what each choice breeds — Haiku's 5x step at 100k (R3) is a trap that springs in ~9 working days; deepseek off-peak half (R10) breeds time-of-day scheduling; gpt-6.1-sol can't hold 1M (R15) and Codex is out of quota.

When revisited: (i) when the per-question loaded set crosses 100k tokens (Haiku step, R3; context-rot caution, R5) — the trigger; (ii) at ~200-300k projected load; (iii) if monthly spend exceeds a set cap; (iv) at 1M projected (80-100 days, R15 eliminates gpt-6.1-sol). Also revisit if a same-window quality measurement appears for deepseek/Gemini (the gap closes).

Hold on: the loaded per-question set. The design loads the index + related categories, not all 62. Today 25k total; if categories are ~1/3, loaded ≈ 8-10k. So growth to 100k loaded is far off. But the brief frames growth to 1M as the whole file. The plan says a 1M window matters "as they grow". So the revisit trigger should be on the loaded set and the total.

Let me also give the cost per question: Sonnet at 25k in ≈ $0.05; with cache hit 5% → $0.0025 for the cached prefix + full price for question/answer. I should be careful to label it as the researcher's arithmetic (context-windows.md digest), not measured.

Now let me also handle "proposed change to the pattern" — a point I may improve. The pattern: time set at start; small pieces timestamped; vision/use cases first; research not recall; workflows. My proposed change could be: the insights index should carry the category as a field and the load decision should be a step in the brief template (so the "before an expert answers" load is checkable). Or: at the start, name the categories; at the end, log which insight ids were cited and whether the failure repeated. That's U2's test. I can propose: the round should record "insight id cited → did the failure repeat" as a piece, so U2 is measurable.

Actually, a good proposed change to the pattern: add to the time-box the rule that a decision made provisionally from expert advice gets an explicit "revisit trigger" field, so that the main agent's provisional call is bound to a condition. My #13: not every command accepted; #5: stop when not advantageous. But is that needed? Maybe.

