## How I decided
1. Reckon before committing (my #1). I ran the numbers the question gave against the files: the digest's 62 lines carry 22/18/14/8 by Source — that matches. But 4 lines name files not in the tree (`runs/20261014-code-map-build/insight.md` for F-60cad515; `runs/20261016-four-experts-what-counts-as-change/insight.md` for F-e82a6f58, F-2cc835e3, F-15cf1278), and 2 headers carry two ids each (F-2cdebf3b+F-d0736dc7; F-c055f734+F-e1161864). So 6 of 64 ids need a validity check before any placement. Basis: my measurement of the digest against `runs/**/insight.md`, not recall. 「夫未戰而廟算勝者，得算多也…」
2. Place by one recorded field, not a second judgment (my #20, my #14). The `Source:` line is written on every section and gives exactly one value; the "land unit" is already recorded. Splitting where the mass is — instruction 22, check 18 — and leaving tool 14 and config 8 whole is "concentrate strength" (my #14), not symmetry.
3. Define the split mechanically. instruction: does the root cause name a file/path/artifact that was missing, refused, stale or changed? yes → **handoff**, no → **brief**. check: did the check fail or log failure on work that was correct? yes → **check-noisy**, no → **check-blind**. tool → **engine-output**; config → **environment**. my #16 (orders clear first): the rule must be writable so two readers place the same section the same way.
4. Not by task type (my reading). The task type is a property of the *question*, not of the section; the insight files show orchestration failures (a brief, a check, a call) that recur across task types. Keep task type as the question's label that picks categories.
5. Pick related with both (my #4, my #17). The main agent knows the self-side: it knows whether it is about to write a brief, run a check, call an engine, or touch config, so it names the categories. Dave reads the index for the other-side signs: 「故策之而知得失之計，作之而知識動靜之理」 — the index (file, id, check, source, option kinds; plan A Part C step 9) lets him pull a section whose check matches even when the asker guessed the category wrong.
6. Do not rely on the asker alone (my #2): 「無恃其不來，恃吾有以待之；無恃其不攻，恃吾有所不可攻也。」 The index read is the preparation you own (≤ about 1k tokens for 62 lines, my arithmetic), and the body loads are the category read.
7. Cost of the load is the real limit, not the window (my #10, my #8). 25k in a 1M window is 2.5% (my arithmetic from the brief's measurement); the harm is context rot (R5) and the price step, not capacity. So load index + category bodies, not the file.
8. Decision 2: reckon the engines on the matters that matter (my #1). Window: all hold 25k easily and all list about 1M (R1, R6, R9, R11) except gpt-6.1-sol, whose input caps at 922k (R15). Price: Sonnet $2/$10 with no step across the window (R1, R4); deepseek-flash ~$0.0075 per 25k peak, half off-peak (R9, R10); Gemini 3.8 Flash ~$0.019 per 25k to 2026-12-31 (R7); Opus $4/$20 (R1); Haiku steps 5x above 100k (R3). Quality: only Anthropic states the long-context behaviour, and it is a warning (R5); Google, DeepSeek and OpenAI give none (context-windows Gaps). Availability: Codex 0% quota today.
9. Choose the capable-but-flat engine, not the cheapest (my #2, my #4). The experiment measures rules+insights (Dave) against a plain control (Dario) on the same engine; a weak or unmeasured engine floors both and puts the difference into the engine, not the rules. Sonnet 5.5 is 1M with no step (R2, R4), cache reads at 5% (R1), ≈$0.05 per 25k call and ≈$0.0025 for a cached prefix (researcher arithmetic, R1).
10. Cache the fixed prefix and keep a fallback (my #2, my #8). Cache reads at 5% make the repeated index+category prefix nearly free; the marginal cost is question+answer. If the engine is unavailable, fall back to deepseek-flash, already the multi-step-planning default (docs/TASK_TYPES.md).
11. Revisit on measured triggers, not on a date (my #13, my #5). Revisit when the per-question loaded set crosses 100k tokens (Haiku's 5x step R3; context rot R5) or the whole file approaches 922k input (R15) or 1M; also if a long-context quality measurement appears for deepseek or Gemini (the research gap), or Codex returns — gpt-6.1-sol can never hold the 1M target (R15).
12. Test against the 62 (the brief's own test). Only ~25 lines carry a title, so the title test runs on those; Source places all 62. Sections that fit two, with my tie-break named: F-60cad515 (Source tool; also a handoff; its file is missing); F-3b8bdcec (Source check; also environment — a quota); F-76013d09 (Source config; also check-noisy); F-76d8d965 (Source config; also engine-output — exit 0 lies); F-5e6801ed and F-cf7d58f7 (Source instruction; also engine-output — missing result file; Source tie-break → handoff); F-969500fa (Source check; also engine-output — a chain hides it); F-90389583 (Source instruction; also environment — HOME through bun's cache). Sections that fit no title test: the 37 without titles, placed by Source only.
13. Proposed change to the pattern: record the categories in the question and require each answer to cite `<file>#<id>` or say why none applies — the `learned:` field and `rates` repeat count already exist (plan A Part C steps 11–13). It changes the brief template by one line and the check step by one citation check; it turns U1/U2 from intentions into checks.
14. Mix the harm in (my #6): the scheme's harm is that Source is the origin/detection locus, so a section can sit under a kind that is not the action to change — the named overlaps above are that harm; the index's `check` and option-kind fields are how Dave pulls across.

